    <DataNode elementId="sLea-Antigen" textLabel="a sialyl-Lewis a epitope" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="MNXM166829" dataSource="MetaNetX" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">The |FRAME: sLea-Antigen &quot;sialyl-Lewis a epitope&quot;| (SLe&lt;sup&gt;a&lt;/sup&gt;) was discovered in 1979 with the use of a monoclonal antibody |CITS: [94699]|. While the antigen is believed to play an important role during embryogenesis |CITS: [6381330]|, in adults only low amounts of the antigen are present on normal epithelial cells |CITS: [6751528][6381289]|. When the selectin family of adhesion molecules was described, it was found that SLe&lt;sup&gt;a&lt;/sup&gt;, along with the |FRAME: sLex-Antigen &quot;sialyl-Lewis x antigen&quot;|, is a ligand for E-selectin. Higher expression of the SLe&lt;sup&gt;a&lt;/sup&gt; antigen in adults is strongly associated with cancer. It was found in high amounts on the surface of human pancreatic, colon and gastric cancer cell lines |CITS: [7142214][6188455]| and in human adenocarcinomas of the colon, stomach, pancreas and gall-bladder |CITS: [94699][6751528][2910493]|. The expression of the antigen has been linked to tumor progression. A gradual increase in SLe&lt;sup&gt;a&lt;/sup&gt; was found in colon and rectum during neoplastic transformation and progression |CITS: [2863413]|, and its presence was restricted to tumorigenic and invasive human urothelial cell lines |CITS: [8900435]|. The antigen is responsible for adhesion of colon, pancreas, urinary bladder and gastric cancer cells to endothelium via its interaction with selectin E |CITS: [1716885][7687590][7678075][8900435][9578490]|. The expression level of the SLe&lt;sup&gt;a&lt;/sup&gt; antigen is used as a prognostic factor for colorectal carcinoma, and is associated with high incidence of recurrence and the length of survival time |CITS: [8273774][7697593][9303355]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="sLea-Antigen" />
